<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kubrat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 665</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Khan Kubrat · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dulo-clan">Dulo clan</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kubrat was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dulo-clan">Dulo clan</a> who, in the 630s, threw off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> or Turkic overlordship and united the Onogur, Bulgar and related tribes of the steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a>. Raised partly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and baptised there, he was an ally of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>. After his death his sons divided the people, and one, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparukh</a>, founded the Bulgarian state on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kubrat.jpg" alt="Kubrat" width="189" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dimitar Atanasov Gyudjenov (1891 - 1979) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giudjenov_Kubrat_and_his_sons_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kubrat was the ruler of the Onogur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>. His name is derived from the Turkic words qobrat, meaning to gather, or qurt, wolf. The Nominalia of the Bulgarian khans calls him Kurt, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dulo-clan">Dulo clan</a>, who succeeded Gostun of the Ermi clan and is credited with a reign of 60 years.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> were Turkic nomads who took part in the Hunnic confederation of the 5th century. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s death the tribes that later formed the Bulgars withdrew east into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and Caspian steppe. By the end of the 6th century the western Bulgar tribes had joined the Avar Khaganate, while the eastern Bulgars came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>. Byzantine writers named him in various ways: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor">Theophanes the Confessor</a> called him king of the Onogundur Huns, and Patriarch Nikephoros I called him ruler of the Onuğundur-Bulğars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Kubrat spent his early life at the imperial palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. John of Nikiu, a 7th-century historian, writes that he was baptised there, received into the Christian community in childhood and grew up in the palace, and that great affection and peace prevailed between him and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>. Whether he was a child or a young adult is unclear. His or his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organa">Organa</a>'s conversion is placed around 619, and he may have formed part of a coalition against the Sasanian-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> alliance that Heraclius or Organa had planned.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-i">Nikephoros I</a>, Kubrat revolted successfully against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and concluded a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>, and the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a>, or Magna Bulgaria, was formed. Nikephoros says he died during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Constans II</a> (641 to 668), and that he told his five sons, among them Batbayan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kotrag">Kotrag</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparukh</a>, never to separate their dwellings, so that their power might thrive. The loose tribal union nonetheless broke up under internal tensions and pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> to the east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereshchepina-treasure">Pereshchepina Treasure</a>, found in 1912 by Ukrainian peasants near Poltava, comprises over 50 kg of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> objects of the migration period. Three rings bear monograms, and one is inscribed in Greek with the name Kubrat and the dignity of patrikios, which led scholars to identify the site as his grave. The bracelets show Byzantine influence or workmanship. The latest coins in the hoard date to about 650, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Constans II</a>.</p><p>Reading the Nominalia's 60 years as a lifespan places his death in 653 or 665; historical and archaeological sources place it between 650 and 665, and his birth between 590 and 615 on one theory. Kubrat Knoll on Livingston Island in Antarctica is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kubrat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The revolt of Samo's Slavs around 623 and the rise of <b>Kubrat</b>'s Great Bulgaria in the 630s were both reactions against Avar lordship, and so were linked to the formation of new states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bulgars were a Turkic-speaking steppe people who in the early 7th century formed the confederation known as Old Great Bulgaria under <b>Khan Kubrat</b>, north of the Black Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Turkic-speaking nomadic elites founded it and took control of the lands from the lower Volga to the northern Caucasus and the steppes north of the Black Sea, subjugating or displacing rivals such as the Bulgars of <b>Kubrat</b>'s Old Great Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Others later joined <b>Kubrat's</b> Old Great Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/state/kutrigurs">Kutrigurs</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 630s <b>Kubrat</b> of the Dulo clan threw off the overlordship of the Avars and united the Onogur and Bulgar tribes of the steppe between the Kuban and the lower Dnieper.</blockquote><a href="/state/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a></li><li><blockquote>Byzantine writers record their dealings with Constantinople and their conversion attempts; their leader Organa and his nephew <b>Kubrat</b> had ties to the Byzantine court.</blockquote><a href="/state/onogurs">Onogurs</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phanagoria">Phanagoria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.19,36.83&amp;year=655" aria-label="Phanagoria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5425 569 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Kubrat"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5875 839h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5875 839h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=45.19,36.83&amp;year=655">What was here in 655 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
